1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,700
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:05,970
Towards a Unified View of
Residual Looped Transformers.

3
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:09,880
Linear attention is a memory over tokens.

4
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:14,480
Each token writes its key and value
into a state that is passed along,

5
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:16,480
and later tokens read from it.

6
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:19,300
The residual stream does
the same over depth.

7
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:22,320
Each block writes its
update into the stream,

8
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:25,720
which carries it forward to the
blocks that read from it later.

9
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:26,700
Unrolled,

10
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:29,540
every read is a weighted
sum of what was written,

11
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:33,120
and the standard residual
fixes every weight at one.

12
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,170
In this view,

13
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:38,250
every residual rule sets
the strength of the input,

14
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:39,910
the total weight of the updates,

15
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:41,650
and how that weight is shared.

16
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:45,110
The standard residual gives
every update a weight of one,

17
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:47,510
so the total grows with every pass.

18
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,450
Hyper-Connections give
each stream one weight.

19
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:53,550
But a stream holds the
input together with updates,

20
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:56,330
so the input cannot be
weighted on its own.

21
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:00,740
Attention Residuals weigh the input
and the updates with one softmax.

22
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,870
Their weights add up to one,

23
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,950
so more weight on the updates
leaves less for the input.

24
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,250
Loopback sets the three separately.

25
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,690
The input keeps a weight
of one on its own path.

26
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,910
A decay bounds the total,

27
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:17,830
and attention shares it
across the stored updates.

28
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:21,980
Here are our training
curves at all five scales.

29
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:26,960
Loopback's loss is lower from early
in training all the way to the end.

30
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:33,060
From 191M to 3B parameters,

31
00:01:33,430 --> 00:01:36,821
Loopback reaches lower
loss than the standard

32
00:01:36,821 --> 00:01:41,040
Transformer while using only
40–75% of its parameters.

33
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:47,330
It has the lowest loss and the highest
benchmark average of all nine designs,

34
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:49,490
at all three sizes we tested.

35
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:56,200
We also held the parameter count
fixed and traded width for depth.

36
00:01:56,570 --> 00:01:58,940
As the model gets deeper and narrower,

37
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:01,620
the standard Transformer loses ground,

38
00:02:01,830 --> 00:02:04,680
while Loopback's margin
grows at both scales.

39
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,190
That's Loopback.

40
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:08,530
Fewer parameters,

41
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:09,370
lower loss.

42
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,250
The paper and code are
on our project page.

